        <update>plot tick-failed</update>
      </pen>
      <pen display="Swapped" color="-13345367" interval="1.0" mode="0" legend="true">
        <setup />
        <update>plot tick-swaps</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <plot x="760" y="550" width="445" height="240" display="Market liquidity" xAxis="ticks" yAxis="money units" xMin="0" xMax="100" yMin="0" yMax="20000" autoPlotX="true" autoPlotY="true" legend="true">
      <setup />
      <update />
      <pen display="Cash" color="-955883" interval="1.0" mode="0" legend="true">
        <setup />
        <update>plot sum [cash] of kiosks</update>
      </pen>
      <pen display="E-float" color="-13345367" interval="1.0" mode="0" legend="true">
        <setup />
        <update>plot sum [efloat] of kiosks</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <plot x="270" y="635" width="466" height="155" display="Cash share across kiosks" xAxis="cash / (cash + e-float)" yAxis="kiosks" xMin="0" xMax="1.01" yMin="0" yMax="20" autoPlotX="false" autoPlotY="true" legend="false">
      <setup />
      <update />
      <pen display="kiosks" color="-10899396" interval="0.1" mode="1" legend="false">
        <setup>set-histogram-num-bars 10</setup>
        <update>histogram [cash / (cash + efloat)] of kiosks</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
  </widgets>
  <info>## PURPOSE AND STATUS

An abstract experiment: can immediate cash-for-e-float exchanges reduce failed
customer transactions? All values are illustrative, not fitted to the Tanzania
survey. The network is synthetic, undirected, and fixed during a run. Survey
"trade" responses represent conditional willingness, not observed exchanges.

## QUICK START

Choose a sharing-mode, press Setup, then Go / stop or +100 ticks. Repeat with
another mode and the same seed and parameters. Setup clears all previous output.
Do not change model controls during a comparison run. Only show-balances? is
intended as a live display control; its labels update on the next tick.

## AGENTS, BALANCES, AND DEMAND

Each kiosk starts with starting-balance cash AND starting-balance e-float. Units
are arbitrary; one tick is an abstract batch of requests, not a day or minute.
Each tick contains requests-per-tick independent requests. Each selects a kiosk
uniformly, then an integer amount uniformly from 1 through maximum-request.
Customer accounts and cash are outside the model and assumed sufficient.

Half the kiosks have withdrawal probability withdrawal-share + demand-polarization;
the other half use withdrawal-share - demand-polarization. Probabilities are
clipped to [0, 1]. Thus withdrawal-share is the midpoint, not necessarily the
realized market share when clipping occurs. Kiosk types alternate around the
circle and do not imply geographic clustering. Polarization creates complementary
cash and e-float needs. Demand is independent across requests, with no common shocks.

A successful deposit adds cash and removes equal e-float. A withdrawal removes
cash and adds equal e-float. Requests are processed sequentially. There is no
partial customer service: an unserviceable request fails immediately and is lost.
There are no customer retries, queues, spending, or feedback to future demand.

## SHARING RULE

No sharing: kiosks serve customers using only their own resources.

Network sharing: exchange is possible along an undirected random link, if both
kiosks participate. Each possible pair has independent probability network-density
of a link. Participation is independently drawn with probability
participation-percent / 100; it need not equal that exact fraction in a small market.

All-to-all sharing: every participating pair is eligible, regardless of links.
Set participation-percent to 100 for universal participation.

Only an otherwise failing customer request triggers exchange. The requester seeks
exactly its shortage, paying an equal amount of the opposite resource. Both sides
must retain minimum-reserve of the resource given away after each swap. The
reserve constrains sharing, not customer service. Multiple partners may jointly
cover a shortage, in random order. Availability is checked first: if the entire
request cannot be funded, no swaps take place. This assumes perfect information
and atomic, instantaneous settlement. There are no fees, travel, credit, default,
provider restrictions, external top-ups, learning, or network changes.

The seed reproduces balances, types, participation, network, and demand.
Sharing and topology use local randomness so scenario differences do not alter
the generated customer request sequence. This holds when other controls stay fixed.

## DISPLAY AND OUTPUT DEFINITIONS

Node color shows cash share: orange below 25%, blue above 75%, teal otherwise.
Color indicates imbalance, not an observed failure or a reserve violation.
Small nodes do not participate. Labels show cash / e-float. Circle positions are
for readability and do not represent distance. Network mode displays eligible
links; turquoise links were used this tick. Other modes hide links to avoid clutter.

Value served (%) is 100 * completed value / requested value. Deposit and
withdrawal rates use their own requested-value denominators. Before any demand,
the corresponding rate displays 0 (not an estimated failure rate).
Requests rescued counts customer requests made possible by sharing. Partner swaps
counts bilateral exchanges; one rescued request may require several swaps.
Swapped value counts the cash leg once, not cash plus e-float. It is an internal
flow, not additional customer activity. Customer totals include deposits and
withdrawals and should not be interpreted as GDP, spending, or unique money.

The histogram shows the distribution of cash shares. Market liquidity shows total
cash and e-float. A swap preserves both market totals; customer transactions
convert one kiosk resource into the other. Cash + e-float remains constant.
check-accounting runs every tick to reject negative or unreconciled balances.

## USEFUL EXPERIMENTS

1. Compare all three modes over 100 ticks with identical settings and seed.
2. Increase network-density or participation-percent to explore access limits.
3. Raise minimum-reserve to examine the cost of protecting potential donors.
4. Set demand-polarization to 0 to remove persistent local demand differences.
5. Set withdrawal-share to 1 and demand-polarization to 0: sharing cannot cure
   a sustained market-wide cash shortage without external replenishment.
6. Repeat across seeds. Better connectivity need not improve every single run:
   exchanges can change which customers are served later.

## DEVELOPMENT BOUNDARY

No real network data are imported. Future extensions can replace setup topology
and balances, add provider compatibility, observed demand and rebalancing costs,
or persistent customers. Each extension requires an explicit new assumption.
</info>
